## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a compliance feature into a concrete, time-bound cost-benefit analysis suitable for a beginner. It tests how assumptions drive ROI and how to articulate go/no-go criteria.

## Key Concepts

- Cost estimation basics for small features
- ROI calculation and break-even timing
- Risk penalties avoided and adoption uplift
- Simple, testable calculations and trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function breakEvenMonths(laborRatePerHr, weeks, engineers, monthlyOverhead, annualBenefit) {
  const upfront = laborRatePerHr * weeks * engineers * 40;
  const monthlyBenefit = annualBenefit / 12;
  const netMonthlyBenefit = monthlyBenefit - monthlyOverhead;
  if (netMonthlyBenefit <= 0) return Infinity;
  return Math.ceil(upfront / netMonthlyBenefit);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust for ARR vs one-time benefits?
- What metrics would you track after launch to validate forecasts?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, new angle on CBA: on-device prefetching to cut latency, balancing storage, energy, privacy, and business impact. Tests the ability to translate UX improvements into measurable costs and decision criteria.

## Key Concepts

- On-device predictor energy/compute
- Cache budgeting and invalidation strategies
- Data transfer vs. storage costs
- Privacy/opt-in and data minimization
- Latency impact and engagement metrics
- Break-even analysis and go/no-go thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
function calcBreakeven(params){
  const {users, cacheMB, energyPerHit, savedMsPerHit, valuePerMs, pricePerMB} = params;
  const energyCost = energyPerHit * users;
  const dataValue = savedMsPerHit * users * valuePerMs;
  const breakevenMonths = (cacheMB * pricePerMB) / ((dataValue) - energyCost);
  return breakevenMonths;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the model without A/B tests for small pilots?
- What privacy constraints would you enforce for on-device prefetching data?
